WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.700
ED GLAESER: Here in New York, we're in city that is stupor mundi--

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:04.950
that is a wonder of the world.

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:08.490
And it is a city in which the physical layout of the city is also a wonder.

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:11.670
And in some sense, it reminds us of how extremely difficult

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:14.400
it is to handle eight plus million people

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:18.210
on this very sort of narrow amount of land.

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:21.439
And infrastructure makes that possible.

00:00:21.439 --> 00:00:22.480
Help us think through it.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:25.755
So what are the problems that the New York's most important forms

00:00:25.755 --> 00:00:27.370
of infrastructure are trying to solve?

00:00:27.370 --> 00:00:30.286
KATE ASCHER: Well I guess the most important thing, which goes way back,

00:00:30.286 --> 00:00:32.820
is that New York City is more than just a skinny island.

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:35.640
So you can't fit eight million people on Manhattan.

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:37.920
You can fit it in five boroughs.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:41.100
But those five boroughs are of course not connected by land.

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:44.310
So probably the most important infrastructure in New York

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:47.850
is actually the bridges and tunnels that connect and weave it together

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:51.209
between Brooklyn, Queens, and Manhattan, between Bronx and Manhattan,

00:00:51.209 --> 00:00:53.250
and of course between Staten Island and Brooklyn.

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:55.530
So without the bridges and the tunnels, you really

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:57.342
don't have a city that could actually work.

00:00:57.342 --> 00:00:58.300
GLAESER: Five boroughs.

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:01.080
Absolutely which reminds us the water is both New York's blessing,

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:02.760
but also its curse.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:06.617
So it starts right with the need to get goods into and out of the city,

00:01:06.617 --> 00:01:07.950
right, because this is a harbor.

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:09.600
I guess it starts with the wharfs.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:12.917
Can you tell us a bit about 17th and 18th century wharfs?

00:01:12.917 --> 00:01:15.000
ASCHER: Well New York really started as a port.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.190
I mean most of the oldest cities in the world

00:01:18.190 --> 00:01:20.310
started as ports because trade was so important.

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:25.800
But it literally was just a jumping off point for the Dutch West India Company

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:27.590
because they wanted to go and trade.

00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:29.574
And so this was a convenient harbor.

00:01:29.574 --> 00:01:30.990
And everything started from there.

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:33.573
The harbor started in a place where you would not know it now,

00:01:33.573 --> 00:01:36.060
it started on the Lower East Side and around where

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:38.580
the seaport is, which is now a tourist attraction as opposed

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:40.410
to a working seaport.

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:43.620
But in the 18th century, and certainly in the 19th century,

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:47.040
there was a dock and a wharf at the end of every street,

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:48.300
if you can imagine that.

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:51.359
Partly for freight, partly for people that were also moving across

00:01:51.359 --> 00:01:54.150
from New Jersey to New York, or from Brooklyn to New York, because,

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:56.220
of course, at that time there were no bridges and tunnels.

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:58.860
GLAESER: Then gradually we started having horse-drawn buses

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:01.990
and other forms of transportation that would move you uptown,

00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:02.974
move the north south.

00:02:02.974 --> 00:02:04.140
ASCHER: Correct, correct.

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:07.780
So the horse drawn carriages, private cars, stagecoaches,

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:09.780
started with stagecoaches, moved to street cars,

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:13.360
eventually they actually ran in tracks on the road,

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:16.242
was wonderful except that there were no rules.

00:02:16.242 --> 00:02:19.200
So you had people's private carriages, and then you had the streetcars,

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:23.490
and then you eventually would have the steam drawn vehicles down the street.

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:27.510
And at some point somebody realized, that was totally, totally chaotic.

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:30.640
Well in the middle of the 19th century, because of course, steam

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:33.030
you know from the steamboat, and then steam came in,

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:36.210
and steam was not only powered things on the streets.

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:38.220
Ultimately the first elevated trains, when

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:40.080
people decided the streets were too crowded,

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:42.150
they needed to put some mobility up.

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:44.400
The original elevated trains were, of course steam

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:49.520
as well, which was very dirty, and very noisy, and very sad for the people who

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:51.097
were walking underneath them.

00:02:51.097 --> 00:02:53.430
So you can imagine that the streets became more chaotic.

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:56.220
And then you added a whole other layer on top

00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:57.740
before you added a layer underground.

00:02:57.740 --> 00:02:59.740
GLAESER: Cars also came to the city.

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:04.640
And, you know, were a source of both mobility and continual heartache.

00:03:04.820 --> 00:03:06.400
Tell me about that.

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:08.800
How did the city deal with cars?

00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:10.420
How did they revolutionize this.

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:12.780
And of course, this is a lot of what Robert Moses is associated with.

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:14.760
ASCHER: It's a good chapter in a bad chapter in some sense.

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:16.551
I mean Robert Moses was famous for building

00:03:16.551 --> 00:03:18.720
most of the highways, and bridges, and parkways.

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:20.803
Originally, remember he was Commissioner of parks.

00:03:20.803 --> 00:03:23.457
So he did parkways that allowed people to recreate.

00:03:23.457 --> 00:03:24.540
It wasn't to get anywhere.

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:27.081
It was to get out of the city and enjoy the green spaces that

00:03:27.081 --> 00:03:28.200
were around the city.

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:31.880
They ultimately became highways and roadways that

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:34.107
were much more functional in purpose.

00:03:34.107 --> 00:03:35.940
So those roadways not only ringed Manhattan,

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:40.450
they went out to Long Island, they went in all sorts of places.

00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:44.070
And they great until the 1950s, 1960s, and thanks

00:03:44.070 --> 00:03:46.890
to a lot of other federal government policies, they

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:51.300
actually allowed the suburbs to grow and New York City to somewhat hollow out,

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:54.330
certainly Manhattan, to hollow out during that period of time

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:57.735
when the city really needed residents and the taxes that those brought.

00:03:57.735 --> 00:04:00.860
So those roads were great, in terms of allowing people to get to the beach,

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:03.660
and to the great green spaces beyond.

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:07.080
They were not so great as sort of vehicles

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:10.460
for, essentially, promoting growth in the suburbs.

